- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= ACTION operation_symbol "(" action_arg_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?
action_arg_list ::= action_arg ("," action_arg)*
action_arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — ACTION is the only external-state construct. The exact operation glossary entry supplies its complete ordered schema, required and optional fields, allowed value categories, result type, and canonical selection rule. Fields must appear once and in that entry's stated order; omitted optional fields have only the entry's stated default, otherwise no default exists. Unknown, duplicate, out-of-order, or type-incompatible fields are static errors. Source-location discovery uses op_find; possession-sensitive household manipulation requires op_pick_up or op_remove; requested generated text uses GENERATE, not ACTION.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol "(" utterance_arg_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION)?
utterance_arg_list ::= utterance_arg ("," utterance_arg)*
utterance_arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — UTTER records a non-effectful symbolic request, answer, revision, or question and is legal only inside a TURN. Its intent entry supplies the exact ordered schema. The optional binding is the uttered PROPOSITION and can be referenced by later turns only through an explicit generated or conversational artifact reference mechanism; it never binds prose. A request for an answer, recipe, explanation, or script is represented by an intent proposition, while the requested artifact itself is represented by GENERATE.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" generation_arg_list ")" "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION
generation_arg_list ::= "attr_specification" "=" proposition ("," "attr_reference" "=" HANDLE)?` — GENERATE creates a symbolic artifact proposition from int_generate_artifact or another glossary-defined generation specification. attr_specification is required and must have artifact, specification, and any required audience/register fields supplied by its intent schema. attr_reference is optional and must be a prior REF[PROPOSITION] when the artifact answers, revises, continues, or derives from an earlier generated artifact. GENERATE is selected canonically for requested artifacts; UTTER is selected for recorded conversational acts; ACTION is selected for actual external effects.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE "(" task_arg_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?
task_arg_list ::= task_arg ("," task_arg)*
task_arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT handle, identical to a declared TASK or CONVO handle. TASK contains executable agent work; UTTER is not a TASK step. ANCHOR is required exactly when source interpretation contains a relative date, and is the translator's fixed date resolution record. Task-call schemas use ordered attribute symbols and the declared callee signature. All declaration, parameter, call, and result names are canonical handles.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- bind sites: action_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; task_call_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; generation_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION` — Bind assigns a typed value to a canonical opaque handle. Handles carry no English-derived descriptive meaning and must follow the global allocation rule. A binding may copy a visible REF but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity symbol, literal, or proposition. Shadowing, use before introduction, and type mismatch are static errors.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable "{" step+ "}"
iterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit
quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check` — FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain finite-list typing, sequential order, lexical scope, and no-shadowing. Their introduced names are canonical handles. The iterable must be LIST[T], and every list element must itself be a valid closed-vocabulary value.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*
conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*
negation ::= NOT negation | primary
primary ::= "(" check ")" | quantifier | comparison
comparison ::= value (comp_op value)?
comp_op ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="` — Check is pure and has fixed precedence OR, AND, NOT, comparison. Operands are only closed Types & Lexicon values. NUMBER supports all comparisons; BOOL, ENTITY, ROLE, VALUE, PROPOSITION, CODE, REF, and LIST support only equality and inequality unless their exact glossary entry explicitly defines an ordering relation. Proposition equality compares intent symbol and ordered field sequence recursively. No text, unbound name, or undeclared ontology symbol can be tested.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER "=" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? "{" utterance+ "}"
speaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT` — Conversation is an ordered record of symbolic communicative acts. TURN bodies contain UTTER only; executable work belongs in TASK and requested artifact production belongs in GENERATE. REPLY_TO names an earlier turn in the same CONVO. REVISES names the current tip of an earlier revision chain and replaces that turn's entire effective body. A follow-up question references a prior generated or uttered proposition through its intent schema's attr_reference field, never through pronouns or prose. Turn handles use canonical preorder allocation.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow_if ::= IF check THEN "{" step+ "}" (ELSE "{" step+ "}")?` — Conditional branching keeps its existing source-order evaluation and child scopes. Its condition is a revised closed-vocabulary Check, and each branch contains only revised TASK step forms. No branch may contain prose, an unbound identifier, or an undeclared ontology symbol.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" generation_arg_list ")" "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION
generation_arg_list ::= "attr_specification" "=" proposition ("," "attr_reference" "=" HANDLE)?` — GENERATE is pure symbolic artifact derivation. Its specification must be a complete glossary-defined generation proposition; an artifact can be an email exchange, recipe, explanation, script, sermon, design recommendation, meal plan, medical explanation, or code artifact only when its specification schema explicitly encodes its participants, constraints, audience, register, relations, and ordered components. Generated content is never a text payload.

