- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE "(" field_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT handle naming a declared TASK or CONVO. TASK contains executable work and symbolic generation, never UTTER. ANCHOR records a required translation-time date resolution whenever the source uses a relative date. Task parameters, calls, and results use handles only; call fields follow declared parameter order and exact types.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- result sites are ACTION -> HANDLE:type, task_call -> HANDLE:type, UTTER -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION, GENERATE -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION` — Bind assigns a typed closed-vocabulary value to a canonical opaque handle. A handle is allocated by the global preorder rule, cannot shadow a visible handle, and cannot be used before its introduction. LET may copy a visible REF of the same type but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity, literal, proposition, or code value.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable "{" step+ "}"
iterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit
quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check` — FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain finite LIST typing, sequential order, lexical scope, and no-shadowing. Their introduced names are canonical handles. Every list element must itself be a closed Types & Lexicon value.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*
conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*
negation ::= NOT negation | primary
primary ::= "(" check ")" | quantifier | comparison
comparison ::= value (comp_op value)?
comp_op ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="` — Check is pure with fixed precedence OR, AND, NOT, comparison. Operands are only closed values. NUMBER supports all comparisons; BOOL, ontology symbols, PROPOSITION, CODE, REF, and LIST support equality and inequality only unless their exact glossary schema defines an ordering. Proposition equality compares intent symbol and ordered fields recursively.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow_if ::= IF check THEN "{" step+ "}" (ELSE "{" step+ "}")?` — Conditional branching retains source-order evaluation and child scopes. Its condition is a closed Check, and each branch can contain only closed TASK step forms.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER "=" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? "{" turn_step+ "}"
