 fully symbolic conversational proposition and is legal only in a TURN. The selected intent schema supplies exact ordered fields. Its optional binding has type PROPOSITION and may be supplied to any later intent or GENERATE a_reference field that permits PROPOSITION; no REF[PROPOSITION] type exists.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask for a high-protein flatbread recipe.", "braincode": "UTTER int_recipe_request(a_target=ent_flatbread,a_nutrient=val_high_protein)->$v3:PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records structured conversational meaning rather than quoted dialogue. Its content is an ordered symbolic intent proposition.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This removes conversational prose and establishes the single proposition identity model used by follow-ups and generation.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= GENERATE \"(\" \"a_specification\" \"=\" proposition (\",\" \"a_reference\" \"=\" HANDLE)? \")\" \"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION", "semantics": "GENERATE purely derives one symbolic artifact proposition. a_specification is required and must be int_generate_artifact or another glossary-defined artifact specification; a_reference is optional, must be a visible PROPOSITION handle, and denotes the artifact or request being answered, continued, or revised. GENERATE always binds PROPOSITION. It is selected for requested artifacts, UTTER for dialogue records, and ACTION for external effects. It is legal in TASK and TURN bodies, allowing a conversation to contain its requested symbolic artifact.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Generate a recipient response to an earlier email exchange.", "braincode": "GENERATE(a_specification=int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_email_exchange,a_specification=int_email_exchange(a_sender=role_other_party,a_recipient=role_user,a_topic=ent_collaboration,a_relation=val_next_recipient_response),a_reference=$v2),a_reference=$v2)->$v3:PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate derives a structured artifact from a structured symbolic specification. It has one proposition result and never accepts or produces prose text.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This merges the conflicting prior definitions and gives references, generated results, and conversational generation one consistent type.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (\":\" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= HANDLE \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= RETURN value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation\ntask_call ::= HANDLE \"(\" field_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?", "semantics": "A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT handle naming a declared TASK or CONVO. TASK contains executable work and symbolic generation, never UTTER. ANCHOR records a required translation-time date resolution whenever the source uses a relative date. Task parameters, calls, and results use handles only; call fields follow declared parameter order and exact types.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If the manager has not replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 ANCHOR @d2026-09-30 {\n  ACTION op_check_replied(a_target=role_manager,a_deadline=@d2026-10-02)->$v2:BOOL\n  IF NOT $v2 THEN { ACTION op_send_email(a_recipient=role_manager,a_content=int_follow_up(a_tone=val_polite)) }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is an ordered executable procedure using typed opaque handles and closed symbolic values.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This removes unrestricted task and parameter identifiers while preserving the execution boundary.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= LET HANDLE \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- result sites are ACTION -> HANDLE:type, task_call -> HANDLE:type, UTTER -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION, GENERATE -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION", "semantics": "Bind assigns a typed closed-vocabulary value to a canonical opaque handle. A handle is allocated by the global preorder rule, cannot shadow a visible handle, and cannot be used before its introduction. LET may copy a visible REF of the same type but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity, literal, proposition, or code value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Store an extension request.", "braincode": "LET $v2:PROPOSITION=int_request_extension(a_recipient=role_manager,a_duration=2,a_unit=val_day)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind stores a typed formal value under an opaque handle. Handles are defined addresses, not descriptive words.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Opaque handles close the former free identifier channel while retaining explicit data flow.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Iterate", "grammar": "flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable \"{\" step+ \"}\"\niterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit\nquantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check", "semantics": "FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain finite LIST typing, sequential order, lexical scope, and no-shadowing. Their introduced names are canonical handles. Every list element must itself be a closed Types & Lexicon value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair one at a time.", "braincode": "ACTION op_find(a_target=ent_pillow,a_source=ent_sofa,a_quantity=2)->$v2:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\nFOR EACH $v3 IN $v2 { ACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v3)->$v4:REF[ENTITY] ACTION op_place(a_target=$v4,a_destination=ent_armchair) }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Iterate processes a finite typed list using a formal loop handle. It cannot introduce an undocumented descriptive name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This makes iteration compatible with closed handles and the possession requirement of household actions.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Check", "grammar": "check ::= disjunction\ndisjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*\nconjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*\nnegation ::= NOT negation | primary\nprimary ::= \"(\" check \")\" | quantifier | comparison\ncomparison ::= value (comp_op value)?\ncomp_op ::= \"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \">\" | \">=\"", "semantics": "Check is pure with fixed precedence OR, AND, NOT, comparison. Operands are only closed values. NUMBER supports all comparisons; BOOL, ontology symbols, PROPOSITION, CODE, REF, and LIST support equality and inequality only unless their exact glossary schema defines an ordering. Proposition equality compares intent symbol and ordered fields recursively.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Proceed only if the total exceeds 50 and membership is true.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  LET $v2:NUMBER=65\n  LET $v3:BOOL=TRUE\n  IF $v2>50 AND $v3==TRUE THEN { ACTION op_wait() }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Check forms a true-or-false condition from typed closed values. It cannot inspect unknown text or identifiers.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This prevents conditions from restoring string or identifier escape hatches.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Flow-If", "grammar": "flow_if ::= IF check THEN \"{\" step+ \"}\" (ELSE \"{\" step+ \"}\")?", "semantics": "Conditional branching retains source-order evaluation and child scopes. Its condition is a closed Check, and each branch can contain only closed TASK step forms.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If there is no reply by 6 June, send a polite follow-up.", "braincode": "ACTION op_check_replied(a_target=role_manager,a_deadline=@d2025-06-06)->$v2:BOOL\nIF NOT $v2 THEN { ACTION op_send_email(a_recipient=role_manager,a_content=int_follow_up(a_tone=val_polite)) }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If chooses an executable branch from a formal typed condition.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This revalidates conditional examples against the closed Action and Check forms.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER \"=\" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? \"{\" turn_step+ \"}\"\nturn_step ::= utterance | generation\nspeaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT", "semantics": "Conversation is an ordered record of symbolic communication. TURN bodies may contain UTTER and GENERATE, enabling a requested artifact to be generated in the same conversational context. REPLY_TO and REVISES use earlier TURN handles; REVISES targets only a revision chain's current tip and replaces that turn's complete effective body. A follow-up or generation reference uses a visible PROPOSITION handle through a_reference, never prose or REF[PROPOSITION].", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask for high-protein flatbread, revise to high reviews, then ask for protein and preparation time.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n  TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_recipe_request(a_target=ent_flatbread,a_nutrient=val_high_protein)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n  TURN $v4 SPEAKER=USER REVISES $v2 { UTTER int_recipe_request(a_target=ent_flatbread,a_nutrient=val_high_protein,a_requirement=val_high_reviews)->$v5:PROPOSITION GENERATE(a_specification=int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_recipe,a_specification=int_recipe_request(a_target=ent_flatbread,a_nutrient=val_high_protein,a_requirement=val_high_reviews),a_reference=$v5))->$v6:PROPOSITION }\n  TURN $v7 SPEAKER=USER REPLY_TO $v4 { UTTER int_nutrition_question(a_reference=$v6,a_nutrient=val_protein) UTTER int_duration_question(a_reference=$v6,a_measure=val_preparation_time) }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records ordered symbolic requests, corrections, and generated artifacts. Follow-ups use typed proposition handles instead of quoted wording.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This provides the missing canonical bridge from conversational request to generation and resolves all proposition-reference mismatches.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}]
Critic's decision: needs-rework
Critic's reasoning: This attempt makes substantial, sound progress on the focus note by closing free-text and free-identifier channels, so N1 is now upheld in the grammar. It cannot be accepted because the narrow replacement ontology causes broad coverage and richness regressions, including most sampled open tasks and both SWE-bench fixes, and because several previously required concrete schema additions remain incomplete. The core direction is sound, but the next revision must expand closed symbolic composition rather than rely on unsupported approximations.
Required changes (address every one explicitly): [{"target": "ontology-symbol", "change": "Replace grouped and partly aspirational domain coverage with independently lookupable category entries for entity-symbol, role-symbol, each attribute, and each value category. For each category state membership rule, canonical synonym mapping, subtype, permitted consuming fields, and a worked example; retain N6's complete-glossary assumption only for missing members of these already-defined categories."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Add closed schemas and canonical selection rules for the missing demonstrated operations: op_rinse, op_slice, filtering by genre/category, and sorting/ranking by a defined criterion. Add typed relational propositions such as genre, rating/order, location, and source so web tasks do not require invented attributes."}, {"target": "code-form and SWE-bench coverage", "change": "Add int_bug_report and compositional CODE schemas for module/class/function/member identifiers, keyword arguments, booleans, assignment, exception/observed-versus-expected facts, calls, tests, and structured replacement/insertion. Define a closed code-atom membership rule or exact atomic code-token category sufficient to encode both sampled fixes without prose."}, {"target": "artifact specifications", "change": "Add symbolic artifact/specification schemas for at least story, short constrained text, technical introduction, legal contract, structured plan/schedule, financial advice, and comparison/recommendation requests. Model participants, relations, ordered items, exact word/count constraints, audience, genre, and requested content propositions symbolically rather than replacing them with int_explain."}, {"target": "int_plan_trip", "change": "Make unspecified trip fields optional or introduce a minimal trip-request schema. Do not require origin, budget, currency, and criterion when the source does not state them; define canonical defaults only if a default has explicit semantics."}, {"target": "N7 canonical selection rules", "change": "For every overlapping intent and operation family, publish a deterministic construct-selection table and canonical field/value mappings. It must resolve recommendation versus explanation, quality/ranking criteria, source versus location, singular versus list result, and artifact generation versus utterance."}, {"target": "attribute-symbol and value subcategories", "change": "Complete the previous required change by giving each listed attribute and each value subcategory its own lookupable glossary entry rather than one bulk member declaration. Explicitly define values needed by the supplied schemas, including depth/length, freshness, availability, quality/ranking, relation, artifact, criterion, capability, and code-AST value categories."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Revalidate all proposal examples and all corpus translations against exact-member legality. Do not use purported symbols such as val_story, val_legal_document, val_channel_plan, val_text, or val_capability_converse unless their exact glossary entries and consuming schemas have been added."}]
Logic issues: ["The ontology entry calls its listed value symbols 'Exact members' while multiple required artifact and semantic categories have no member or schema; the future-member rule does not make an absent artifact specification legal today.", "op_find's a_requirement accepts only PROPOSITION, but no supplied proposition schema expresses high rating, comedy genre, audience-score ordering, or similar common search constraints.", "The proposed household vocabulary omits op_rinse and op_slice although both are necessary in sampled ALFRED/seed trajectories and were part of the earlier language's coverage.", "The claimed SWE-bench code coverage is not achieved: the finite supplied code atoms cannot express either sampled patch, and int_bug_report is absent despite being required previously.", "int_plan_trip has required origin and budget fields, making a simple destination-and-duration request unfaithful unless a translator invents information.", "The worked Conversation example is internally legal on proposition handles, but its artifact/reference design does not solve the broader absence of schemas for most generated artifact content.", "The current grouped attribute list is not independently lookupable per attribute or value category, so it does not fully satisfy the prior required change or N2's glossary organization requirement."]
Pre-KPI assessment: {"coverage": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "The closed symbolic basis improves representability for a narrow set of household, email, recipe, and simple recommendation tasks, but removes the current language's ability to retain the actionable core of most sampled web, SWE-bench, planning, and open-generation tasks. Missing operations and schemas include sorting/filtering, rinsing/slicing, code bug reports and arbitrary structured edits, legal documents, channel schedules, mortgage advice, and story generation."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Typed propositions, dates, paths, quantities, handles, and revision links preserve more structure than prior strings where a supplied schema fits. However, most sample meanings cannot be represented without omitting essential relations, constraints, requested artifact form, or substantive content; under N4 those omissions are failures rather than permissible opaque payloads."}, "determinism": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "Canonical handles, ordered schema fields, typed values, and an explicit structural vocabulary materially reduce prior identifier and string variation. Determinism remains incomplete because the ontology does not define canonical construct selection for many meanings and providers disagree on operation/field choice, such as source versus location, recommendations versus explain requests, and unsupported code edits."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Closed literal forms and typed schemas are easier to inspect than free strings, and the revised grammar distinguishes source notation from source tokens. But densely grouped glossary entries obscure individual attribute/category meanings, schemas omit many needed concepts, and the claimed complete ontology is not actually published, so a reader cannot recover omitted task meaning from many expressions."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "For supported operational tasks, explicit find/pick-up/place sequencing and typed conversational propositions would aid a weaker agent. For unsupported tasks, the only legal approximation loses the requested work, so the expression would make an agent worse than the original request."}}
Simulated examples that exposed gaps: [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_pillow,a_source=ent_sofa,a_quantity=2)->$v2:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\nFOR EACH $v3 IN $v2 { ACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v3)->$v4:REF[ENTITY] ACTION op_place(a_target=$v4,a_destination=ent_armchair) }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action and Iterate support this task, assuming the complete entity category contains the three entity symbols as N6 directs."}, {"item_id": "dev-embodied-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Rinse the mug in the sink, then put it in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_mug,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_place(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_coffee_maker)\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The closest legal program omits rinsing. op_rinse is absent from the proposed closed operation vocabulary, so the actionable core is not representable."}, {"item_id": "86afd67c-1bff-455c-baa7-e18dcb64b0f3", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Find comedy tv shows on netflix sorted by audience score.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_tv_show,a_source=ent_netflix,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "op_find has neither genre nor ordering fields, and no op_sort schema exists. The legal approximation loses comedy, audience-score ordering, and plural result intent."}, {"item_id": "bf469f30-6628-4017-b963-672645d7feab", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Find the highest rated dog collar under 10 dollar.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_dog_collar,a_max_price=10,a_currency=val_usd,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Price is representable, but no proposition or canonical sorting operation encodes highest rated. val_high_reviews cannot occur directly in op_find's a_requirement field because that field requires PROPOSITION."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190907_223758_523581#1", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Microwave a slice of apple and place it on the table.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_apple_slice,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_heat(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_microwave)->$v4:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_remove(a_target=$v4,a_source=ent_microwave)->$v5:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_place(a_target=$v5,a_destination=ent_table)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The requested end-state is represented, but the demonstrated trajectory requires slicing an apple and no op_slice exists. The language cannot distinguish an existing slice from a slice that must be created."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_224836_919680#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Put a microwaved slice of apple on the table in the corner.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_find(a_target=ent_apple_slice,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_heat(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_microwave)->$v4:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_remove(a_target=$v4,a_source=ent_microwave)->$v5:REF[ENTITY]\nACTION op_place(a_target=$v5,a_destination=ent_corner_table)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "This captures heating and placement if ent_corner_table is a complete-glossary entity, but it cannot encode that the slice must be produced from an apple when that procedural requirement is intended."}, {"item_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-11578", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Fix LogisticRegressionCV multinomial probabilistic scoring so the scoring LogisticRegression receives multi_class and related constructor parameters.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_modify_code(a_path=@psklearn/linear_model/logistic.py,a_change=code_edit_replace(a_target=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_x),a_value=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_x)))\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The only supplied code atoms cannot encode LogisticRegression, multi_class, fit_intercept, constructor arguments, scoring behavior, regression facts, or a test. The legal expression is semantically vacuous."}, {"item_id": "django__django-13112", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Fix makemigrations crashing for a ForeignKey with a mixed-case Django app name.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\nACTION op_modify_code(a_path=@pdjango/db/models/fields/related.py,a_change=code_edit_replace(a_target=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_x),a_value=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_x)))\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No symbolic bug-report, mixed-case-name, ForeignKey, lazy-reference, exception, or test schema exists. The code AST is too small to state the requested fix."}, {"item_id": "c6516", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Can you give me some great places to visit in the Netherlands?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_recommend(a_target=ent_place,a_location=ent_netherlands)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The recommendation and location are represented, but no schema captures the requested quality criterion \"great\" or the desired plurality/list of destinations."}, {"item_id": "c3388", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "hi how hs your day been?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_capability_question(a_subject=role_agent,a_capability=val_capability_converse)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "This is only a legal conversational approximation. There is no greeting, wellbeing inquiry, social-response, or day-status proposition, and val_capability_converse is not among the supplied exact value members."}, {"item_id": "c2067", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Can you breifly explain American politics to me?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_american_politics,a_audience=role_user)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "int_explain captures the topic and audience, but the requested brevity has no defined depth value; val_simple does not mean brief."}, {"item_id": "c2904", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Tell me about what is happening in New Zealand Politically", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_new_zealand_politics,a_audience=role_user)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The topic is representable under the assumed entity category, but the current-events/freshness requirement has no symbolic field or value."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-73283", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Introduce the topic of the impact of dam construction on seasonal precipitation, land use/cover, and socio-economic conditions in Eastern Sudan.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_dam_construction,a_audience=role_user)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The approximation drops the requested introductory genre and every required relation: seasonal precipitation, land use/cover, socio-economic impact, and Eastern Sudan. N4 forbids retaining them as prose."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-76731", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Which Dostoevsky work has the most parallels with The Holy Blood and the Holy Grail?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_opinion_request(a_topic=ent_dostoevsky_work)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "There is no comparison, literary-work relation, thematic-parallel, or constrained recommendation schema. The expression loses both compared works and the requested criterion."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-137561", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write a story of when Gloopy and Glitter met a scientist who was amazed that they could speak.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_story,a_specification=int_explain(a_topic=ent_gloopiter))->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "val_story is not a supplied exact member and int_explain is not a legal artifact-specification schema for story generation. Characters, meeting event, scientist, speech, and amazement lack symbolic representation."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-78000", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write me a legal document and contract for a high-demand speaker saying there is not enough time and the speaker is in very high demand.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_legal_document,a_specification=int_explain(a_topic=ent_speaking_engagement))->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No legal-contract artifact/specification schema, parties, obligations, signature, availability, or high-demand constraint exists. The shown values are not supplied exact glossary members."}, {"item_id": "1776009617894", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Plan engaging and useful topics for the first 5 videos of an educational YouTube channel for children called Hamoudi.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_channel_plan,a_specification=int_explain(a_topic=ent_education))->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "There is no channel-plan artifact, video/topic sequence, child-audience category, name-bearing channel entity, five-item constraint, or engagement/usefulness criterion."}, {"item_id": "1775429344935", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Help me create a 100-word text about my decision to go to Japan to learn about SGI.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_text,a_specification=int_explain(a_topic=ent_sgi))->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The proposal has no text artifact, first-person decision/travel relation, Japan/SGI learning relation, or exact 100-word constraint. The illustrated expression is not valid under the listed exact value members."}, {"item_id": "1775752130482", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Hello, I want to plan a 3 day safari in eastern cape south africa.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_plan_trip(a_destination=ent_eastern_cape_south_africa,a_duration=3,a_unit=val_day,a_interest=ent_safari,a_origin=ent_unspecified,a_budget_amount=0,a_budget_currency=val_usd,a_requirement=val_simple)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "int_plan_trip unnecessarily requires origin, budget, currency, and criterion, forcing invented values absent from the request. The shown placeholders are therefore not a faithful encoding."}, {"item_id": "1775479438859", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "How do I get a job contract to access mortgage products rather than paying cash in a city with high unemployment and cheap properties?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\nTURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_mortgage_employment)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The expression omits job-contract acquisition, mortgage qualification, cash-versus-financing comparison, local unemployment, and low property cost; there is no financial-advice or causal-planning schema."}]
