- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE "(" field_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT HANDLE naming a declared TASK or CONVO. TASK contains ordered external work, control flow, binding, task calls, and symbolic generation, but never UTTER. ANCHOR is required whenever source meaning uses a relative date and carries the resolved DATE_LITERAL. Parameters, task names, calls, and results use handles only; calls use declared ordered fields and exact types.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- Result sites: ACTION -> HANDLE:type; task_call -> HANDLE:type; UTTER -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION; GENERATE -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION.` — Bind assigns a typed closed value to an opaque handle. Handles follow the global preorder allocation rule, cannot shadow visible handles, and cannot be used before their introduction. LET may copy a visible REF of exactly the same type but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity symbol, literal, proposition, or code value.
