TASK Deprecated {}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 2, version 2.0.0*

### `Generate`

**Grammar:** `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" gen_attr_list? ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
gen_attr_list ::= gen_attr ("," gen_attr)*
gen_attr ::= "quantity" "=" UNSIGNED_INT | IDENT "=" value
UNSIGNED_INT ::= "0" | [1-9][0-9]*
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type "]"
-- A quantity attribute, if present, must use the quantity production above; quantity=NUMBER through the generic attribute production is a static error.`

**Semantics:** GENERATE is a pure, non-effectful construct that invokes a generative model to produce a value without external side effects. It shares Action's canonical attribute vocabulary and duplicate-attribute prohibition. target is always the requested output artifact, never content; content is the source instruction or text payload; audience is the intended reader; recipient is forbidden. Its optional tone and register_note follow Action's shared tone normalization rules and are mutually exclusive.

A GENERATE result type must be a gen_type: NUMBER, STRING, BOOL, or a finite nesting of LIST around those types. REF[T] is prohibited at every nesting depth: GENERATE may not declare REF[T], LIST[REF[T]], or any type containing REF. This restriction follows the global REF rule: generated text or values cannot manufacture an opaque runtime identity. The generator must return a value exactly matching its declared gen_type, including homogeneous recursively valid list elements.

quantity is an exact output-size constraint, not a maximum, minimum, estimate, or preference. Its lexical form is UNSIGNED_INT only: it may have no sign, decimal point, exponent, whitespace, or leading zero except for the literal 0. quantity is legal only for results declared STRING or LIST[T]. For LIST[T], the result contains exactly quantity elements. For STRING, it contains exactly quantity words. A word is a maximal non-empty sequence of Unicode characters whose General_Category is a Letter category (L*) or Decimal_Number (Nd); every other Unicode character is a separator. Thus quantity=0 on STRING requires a string containing no such sequence, and quantity=0 on LIST[T] requires an empty list. The executor validates the declared type and quantity condition before completion; inability to produce a conforming result fails the step under the general external-failure rule.

GENERATE must always bind its result using -> IDENT : gen_type. Use GENERATE to produce an artifact for the agent environment; use CONVO and UTTER to record observed dialogue or dialogue history.

**Example:**
- NL: "Give the user an opinion about taxes in exactly 50 words."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT TaxesOpinion

TASK TaxesOpinion : STRING {
  GENERATE(target="response", audience="user", content="Respond to the user's view that taxes are bad.", quantity=50) -> response : STRING
  RETURN response
}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 7, version 6.0.0*


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | ENTITY | ROLE | VALUE | PROPOSITION | CODE | CODEATOM | LIST "[" type "]" | REF "[" type "]"
value ::= literal | glossary_symbol | HANDLE | list_lit | proposition | code | diff
literal ::= NUMBER_LITERAL | TRUE | FALSE | DATE_LITERAL | TIME_LITERAL | URL_LITERAL | PATH_LITERAL | NAME_LITERAL
list_lit ::= "[" value ("," value)* "]"
proposition ::= intent_symbol "(" field_list? ")"
code ::= code_constructor "(" field_list? ")"
field_list ::= field ("," field)*
field ::= attribute_symbol "=" value
diff ::= HANDLE "-" NUMBER_LITERAL
HANDLE ::= "$v" POSITIVE_INTEGER
POSITIVE_INTEGER ::= [1-9][0-9]*
NUMBER_LITERAL ::= "-"? ("0" | [1-9][0-9]*) ("." DIGIT+)?
DATE_LITERAL ::= "@d" DIGIT DIGIT DIGIT DIGIT "-" MONTH "-" DAY
TIME_LITERAL ::= "@t" HOUR ":" MINUTE ":" SECOND "Z"
URL_LITERAL ::= "@uhttps://" URL_HOST URL_PATH?
PATH_LITERAL ::= "@p" PATH_SEGMENT ("/" PATH_SEGMENT)*
NAME_LITERAL ::= "@n" NAME_CHAR+
glossary_symbol ::= exact member of entity-symbol | role-symbol | semantic-symbol` — Grammar names are metalanguage, never source tokens. DATE_LITERAL must be a real proleptic-Gregorian date; TIME_LITERAL is UTC; URL_LITERAL is an absolute HTTPS URL; PATH_LITERAL is a normalized relative path; NAME_LITERAL is one atomic token, never a phrase. STRING, comments, unrestricted identifiers, and quoted text are not BrainCode forms. Handles are allocated monotonically in preorder source introduction order and may be used only after introduction. Every proposition and CODE constructor has an exact glossary schema that fixes fields, types, order, and defaults.
- [add vocabulary] structural-symbol (closed): Structural symbols are the complete fixed keywords, operators, delimiters, types, and literal prefixes of BrainCode. They organize expressions but do not carry domain meaning. Members: ENTRYPOINT TASK ANCHOR ACTION UTTER GENERATE CONVO TURN SPEAKER USER AGENT REPLY_TO REVISES IF THEN ELSE FOR EACH IN ANY ALL SATISFIES LET RETURN DECREASES, NUMBER BOOL DATE TIME URL PATH NAME ENTITY ROLE VALUE PROPOSITION CODE CODEATOM LIST REF TRUE FALSE, == != < <= > >= -> = : - ( ) { } [ ] , $v @d @t @u @p @n
- [add vocabulary] entity-symbol (open): Entity symbols name atomic things, places, systems, topics, works, organizations, and named concepts. They are canonical ontology nodes, not English phrases. Members: ent_mug, ent_sink, ent_coffee_maker, ent_pillow, ent_sofa, ent_armchair, ent_apple, ent_microwave, ent_table, ent_tv_show, ent_netflix, ent_dog_collar; membership rule: A complete glossary may add ent_ followed by a canonical lowercase underscore token for one atomic entity or concept. A member must define its subtype as physical-object, place, service, topic, work, organization, or concept; list its canonical natural-language mappings; and state each consuming schema where required. It cannot contain spaces, punctuation, inflection, or embedded prose.
- [add vocabulary] role-symbol (open): Role symbols identify participants by their defined relationship in an interaction or request. They are not free-form names or pronouns. Members: role_user, role_agent, role_manager, role_scientist, role_speaker; membership rule: A complete glossary may add role_ followed by one canonical lowercase underscore token for a stable participant relation. Each member must state its relation subtype, canonical mappings, and schemas that accept ROLE. Personal names use NAME_LITERAL only where a schema explicitly accepts NAME.
- [add vocabulary] semantic-symbol (open): Semantic symbols are the exact operations, intent schemas, attributes, controlled values, code constructors, and code atoms used in BrainCode. Each listed schema fixes field order, permitted types, result type, and canonical use. Members: a_target a_source a_destination a_recipient a_content a_quantity a_requirement a_order a_relation a_location a_duration a_unit a_tone a_depth a_freshness a_audience a_artifact a_specification a_reference a_items a_topic a_participants a_event a_constraint a_path a_change a_regression a_expected a_observed a_exception a_module a_class a_function a_member a_args a_keyword_args a_value a_name, val_day val_usd val_polite val_brief val_in_depth val_current val_high_quality val_highest_rated val_audience_score_desc val_comedy val_genre val_location val_source val_parallel val_meeting val_speaking val_amazement val_child val_engaging val_useful val_first_person val_financing val_cash val_high_unemployment val_low_property_cost val_story val_short_text val_technical_introduction val_legal_contract val_schedule val_trip_plan val_financial_advice val_recommendation val_recipe val_text val_create val_read val_update val_delete val_true val_false, op_find, op_filter, op_sort, op_pick_up op_place op_remove op_heat op_rinse op_slice, op_send_email op_check_replied op_modify_code op_run_tests op_wait, int_genre int_rating int_order int_at_location int_from_source int_parallel int_impact int_event int_property int_comparison, int_request_extension int_follow_up int_recommend int_explain int_social_inquiry int_compare_request int_plan_trip int_financial_advice, int_bug_report int_generate_artifact int_story_spec int_short_text_spec int_technical_intro_spec int_contract_spec int_schedule_spec int_comparison_spec, code_sequence code_identifier code_module code_class code_function code_member code_string code_number code_boolean code_call code_keyword_arg code_assign code_exception code_observed_expected code_replace code_insert code_test_case code_assert_equal, code_atom_LogisticRegressionCV code_atom_LogisticRegression code_atom_multi_class code_atom_fit_intercept code_atom_scoring code_atom_ForeignKey code_atom_makemigrations code_atom_lazy_reference code_atom_Django code_atom_mixed_case code_atom_true code_atom_false code_atom_ValueError code_atom_x; membership rule: Each future member must be an exact canonical token in one declared subtype: operation, intent, attribute, controlled-value category, code constructor, or code atom. A schema member must state ordered fields, permitted types/categories, optionality, duplicates rule, result type, and canonical construct-selection rule. No member may encode a phrase, English syntax, or undeclared free value.
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= ACTION operation_symbol "(" field_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?` — ACTION is the only external-state construct. The selected operation's semantic-symbol entry fixes field order, exact field types, optional fields, result type, and selection rule; missing, duplicate, unordered, unknown, or mistyped fields are static errors. Canonical retrieval is op_find, then op_filter for selection relations, then op_sort for ranking. op_find returns a scalar only when a_quantity is exactly 1; otherwise it returns a list. Household actions requiring possession consume REF[ENTITY]; first acquire it with op_find and op_pick_up. TASK uses ACTION for effects; UTTER records requests and GENERATE produces a symbolic artifact.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol "(" field_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION)?` — UTTER records exactly one non-effectful symbolic proposition and is legal only in a TURN. The chosen intent schema fixes fields and order. Its optional result binds the complete proposition as PROPOSITION for later symbolic reference; it never binds prose content. Use UTTER to record a participant's request, question, correction, greeting, or social inquiry; use GENERATE for a requested artifact and ACTION for an external effect.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" "a_specification" "=" proposition ("," "a_reference" "=" HANDLE)? ")" "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION` — GENERATE is pure symbolic artifact derivation. a_specification is required and must be int_generate_artifact or an artifact-specification intent accepted by that schema; a_reference optionally names a visible PROPOSITION. The result is always PROPOSITION. GENERATE is selected when the request asks the agent to produce a story, text, introduction, contract, schedule, plan, comparison, recommendation, or other artifact; UTTER merely records that the request was made.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE "(" field_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT HANDLE naming a declared TASK or CONVO. TASK contains ordered external work, control flow, binding, task calls, and symbolic generation, but never UTTER. ANCHOR is required whenever source meaning uses a relative date and carries the resolved DATE_LITERAL. Parameters, task names, calls, and results use handles only; calls use declared ordered fields and exact types.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- Result sites: ACTION -> HANDLE:type; task_call -> HANDLE:type; UTTER -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION; GENERATE -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION.` — Bind assigns a typed closed value to an opaque handle. Handles follow the global preorder allocation rule, cannot shadow visible handles, and cannot be used before their introduction. LET may copy a visible REF of exactly the same type but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity symbol, literal, proposition, or code value.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable "{" step+ "}"
iterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit
quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check` — FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL operate only on finite LIST[T] values. Their introduced names are handles, scoped to one loop body or quantifier check and allocated under the global handle rule. Iteration is sequential. Every list element must itself be a legal closed Types & Lexicon value.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*
conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*
negation ::= NOT negation | primary
primary ::= "(" check ")" | quantifier | comparison
comparison ::= value (comp_op value)?
comp_op ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="` — Check is pure and has precedence OR, AND, NOT, comparison. Operands are only closed values. NUMBER, DATE, and TIME support all comparisons; BOOL, glossary symbols, PROPOSITION, CODE, REF, and LIST support equality and inequality only unless a schema explicitly defines ordering. Proposition equality compares its intent symbol and ordered fields recursively.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER "=" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? "{" turn_step+ "}"
turn_step ::= utterance | generation
speaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT` — Conversation is an ordered record of symbolic communication. TURN bodies contain UTTER and GENERATE only. REPLY_TO and REVISES name earlier TURN handles; REVISES may target only a revision chain's current tip and replaces the target's effective body. Follow-ups reference visible PROPOSITION handles through schemas that accept a_reference. Canonical selection is: use UTTER for a participant's request, use GENERATE for the requested artifact, and use ACTION only in TASK for an external effect.

