highest_rated val_audience_score_desc val_comedy val_genre val_location val_source val_parallel val_meeting val_speaking val_amazement val_child val_engaging val_useful val_first_person val_financing val_cash val_high_unemployment val_low_property_cost val_story val_short_text val_technical_introduction val_legal_contract val_schedule val_trip_plan val_financial_advice val_recommendation val_recipe val_text val_create val_read val_update val_delete val_true val_false", "gloss": "Controlled values. Each value is atomic and belongs respectively to duration-unit, currency, tone, depth, freshness, quality/ranking, genre, relation, audience, criterion, artifact, financial-choice, CRUD-operation, or boolean-value subcategories as named by its consuming schema.", "nl_synonyms": ["politely", "briefly", "current", "highest rated", "audience score", "comedy", "great", "for children", "useful", "mortgage", "paying cash"]}, {"symbol": "op_find", "gloss": "Find schema: a_target:ENTITY, optional a_source:ENTITY, optional a_requirement:PROPOSITION, optional a_order:PROPOSITION, required a_quantity:NUMBER. Fields use this order. Returns REF[ENTITY] for quantity 1 and LIST[REF[ENTITY]] otherwise. Use for locating/selecting; source is the searched service or container, location is expressed inside a_requirement.", "nl_synonyms": ["find", "search", "locate"]}, {"symbol": "op_filter", "gloss": "Filter schema: a_target:LIST[REF[ENTITY]], a_requirement:PROPOSITION. Returns the same filtered list. Use once for each source request containing a selection relation after retrieval.", "nl_synonyms": ["filter", "only show"]}, {"symbol": "op_sort", "gloss": "Sort schema: a_target:LIST[REF[ENTITY]], a_order:PROPOSITION. Returns the same list in requested order. Use whenever ranking/order is requested; do not encode ranking as an unstructured find requirement.", "nl_synonyms": ["sort", "rank", "order"]}, {"symbol": "op_pick_up op_place op_remove op_heat op_rinse op_slice", "gloss": "Household schemas: pick_up(a_target:REF[ENTITY]) returns same REF; place(a_target:REF[ENTITY],a_destination:ENTITY|REF[ENTITY]) returns none; remove(a_target:REF[ENTITY],a_source:ENTITY|REF[ENTITY]) returns same REF; heat(a_target:REF[ENTITY],a_destination:ENTITY|REF[ENTITY]) returns same REF; rinse(a_target:REF[ENTITY],a_destination:ENTITY|REF[ENTITY]) returns same REF; slice(a_target:REF[ENTITY],a_quantity:NUMBER) returns REF[ENTITY] for one slice and LIST[REF[ENTITY]] otherwise. Use slice only when the source requires production of slices, not merely selection of an existing slice.", "nl_synonyms": ["pick up", "put", "remove", "microwave", "rinse", "slice", "cut"]}, {"symbol": "op_send_email op_check_replied op_modify_code op_run_tests op_wait", "gloss": "External schemas: send_email(a_recipient:ROLE,a_content:PROPOSITION); check_replied(a_target:ROLE,a_deadline:DATE) returns BOOL; modify_code(a_path:PATH,a_change:CODE,a_regression:CODE optional); run_tests(a_target:CODE); wait(). TASK is chosen for these effects, never UTTER or GENERATE.", "nl_synonyms": ["email", "check reply", "fix code", "run tests", "wait"]}, {"symbol": "int_genre int_rating int_order int_at_location int_from_source int_parallel int_impact int_event int_property int_comparison", "gloss": "Relational propositions: genre(a_target:ENTITY,a_value:VALUE[genre]); rating(a_target:ENTITY,a_value:VALUE[quality]); order(a_target:ENTITY,a_value:VALUE[ranking]); at-location(a_target:ENTITY,a_location:ENTITY); from-source(a_target:ENTITY,a_source:ENTITY); parallel(a_target:ENTITY,a_reference:ENTITY); impact(a_target:ENTITY,a_reference:ENTITY); event(a_participants:LIST[ENTITY|ROLE],a_relation:VALUE[relation]); property(a_target:ENTITY|ROLE,a_value:VALUE); comparison(a_target:PROPOSITION,a_reference:PROPOSITION). These express retrieval constraints and requested relations.", "nl_synonyms": ["comedy", "rated", "audience score", "in", "from", "parallel", "impact", "meeting", "versus"]}, {"symbol": "int_request_extension int_follow_up int_recommend int_explain int_social_inquiry int_compare_request int_plan_trip int_financial_advice", "gloss": "Request schemas: extension(a_recipient:ROLE,a_duration:NUMBER,a_unit:VALUE[duration-unit],a_tone:VALUE[tone] optional); follow-up(a_tone:VALUE[tone]); recommend(a_target:ENTITY,a_location:ENTITY optional,a_requirement:PROPOSITION optional,a_quantity:NUMBER optional); explain(a_topic:ENTITY|PROPOSITION,a_audience:ROLE|ENTITY optional,a_depth:VALUE[depth] optional,a_freshness:VALUE[freshness] optional); social-inquiry(a_recipient:ROLE,a_topic:VALUE); compare-request(a_target:ENTITY,a_reference:ENTITY,a_requirement:PROPOSITION); trip(a_destination:ENTITY,a_duration:NUMBER,a_unit:VALUE[duration-unit],a_requirement:PROPOSITION optional); financial-advice(a_topic:PROPOSITION,a_requirement:PROPOSITION optional). Optional fields are omitted, never filled with invented placeholders.", "nl_synonyms": ["recommend", "explain", "how is your day", "compare", "plan trip", "mortgage advice"]}, {"symbol": "int_bug_report int_generate_artifact int_story_spec int_short_text_spec int_technical_intro_spec int_contract_spec int_schedule_spec int_comparison_spec", "gloss": "Artifact and bug schemas: bug-report(a_target:CODE,a_observed:PROPOSITION,a_expected:PROPOSITION,a_exception:CODE optional); generate-artifact(a_artifact:VALUE[artifact],a_specification:PROPOSITION,a_audience:ROLE|ENTITY optional); story-spec(a_participants:LIST[ENTITY|ROLE],a_event:PROPOSITION,a_constraint:PROPOSITION optional); short-text-spec(a_topic:PROPOSITION,a_quantity:NUMBER,a_audience:ROLE|ENTITY optional); technical-intro-spec(a_topic:PROPOSITION,a_audience:ROLE|ENTITY optional); contract-spec(a_participants:LIST[ROLE|ENTITY],a_items:LIST[PROPOSITION]); schedule-spec(a_target:ENTITY,a_items:LIST[PROPOSITION],a_quantity:NUMBER); comparison-spec(a_target:ENTITY,a_reference:ENTITY,a_requirement:PROPOSITION). Artifact schemas preserve requested content symbolically.", "nl_synonyms": ["bug", "story", "100-word text", "introduction", "contract", "plan", "schedule", "comparison"]}, {"symbol": "code_sequence code_identifier code_module code_class code_function code_member code_string code_number code_boolean code_call code_keyword_arg code_assign code_exception code_observed_expected code_replace code_insert code_test_case code_assert_equal", "gloss": "CODE constructors: sequence(a_items:LIST[CODE]); identifier/module/class/function/member/string/number/boolean(a_value:CODEATOM); call(a_target:CODE,a_args:LIST[CODE],a_keyword_args:LIST[CODE]); keyword-arg(a_name:CODEATOM,a_value:CODE); assign(a_target:CODE,a_value:CODE); exception(a_value:CODEATOM); observed-expected(a_observed:CODE,a_expected:CODE); replace(a_target:CODE,a_value:CODE); insert(a_target:CODE,a_value:CODE); test-case(a_items:LIST[CODE]); assert-equal(a_expected:CODE,a_observed:CODE). These are compositional AST values, not source-text fragments.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "code_atom_LogisticRegressionCV code_atom_LogisticRegression code_atom_multi_class code_atom_fit_intercept code_atom_scoring code_atom_ForeignKey code_atom_makemigrations code_atom_lazy_reference code_atom_Django code_atom_mixed_case code_atom_true code_atom_false code_atom_ValueError code_atom_x", "gloss": "Atomic code tokens. Each has a declared subtype: identifier, class, function, member, keyword, framework, exception, boolean, or test fixture. A complete glossary may add code_atom_ followed by one ASCII underscore token only when it states subtype and canonical code-token mapping; atoms are never concatenated as prose.", "nl_synonyms": ["LogisticRegressionCV", "LogisticRegression", "multi_class", "fit_intercept", "ForeignKey", "makemigrations"]}], "membership_rule": "Each future member must be an exact canonical token in one declared subtype: operation, intent, attribute, controlled-value category, code constructor, or code atom. A schema member must state ordered fields, permitted types/categories, optionality, duplicates rule, result type, and canonical construct-selection rule. No member may encode a phrase, English syntax, or undeclared free value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find comedy TV shows on Netflix sorted by audience score.", "braincode": "ACTION op_find(a_target=ent_tv_show,a_source=ent_netflix,a_quantity=10)->$v1:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\nACTION op_filter(a_target=$v1,a_requirement=int_genre(a_target=ent_tv_show,a_value=val_comedy))->$v2:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\nACTION op_sort(a_target=$v2,a_order=int_order(a_target=ent_tv_show,a_value=val_audience_score_desc))->$v3:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "This expands the exact symbolic ontology with the missing operations, relations, artifacts, values, and compositional code forms while retaining one lookupable controlled category for each source symbol.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= ACTION operation_symbol \"(\" field_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?", "semantics": "ACTION is the only external-state construct. The selected operation's semantic-symbol entry fixes field order, exact field types, optional fields, result type, and selection rule; missing, duplicate, unordered, unknown, or mistyped fields are static errors. Canonical retrieval is op_find, then op_filter for selection relations, then op_sort for ranking. op_find returns a scalar only when a_quantity is exactly 1; otherwise it returns a list. Household actions requiring possession consume REF[ENTITY]; first acquire it with op_find and op_pick_up. TASK uses ACTION for effects; UTTER records requests and GENERATE produces a symbolic artifact.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Rinse the mug in the sink, then put it in the coffee maker.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n ACTION op_find(a_target=ent_mug,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\n ACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY]\n ACTION op_rinse(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_sink)->$v4:REF[ENTITY]\n ACTION op_place(a_target=$v4,a_destination=ent_coffee_maker)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs one glossary-defined external operation. Its operation schema determines exactly which symbolic fields and values are legal.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This removes free Action names and payloads while covering the missing rinse, slice, filtering, ranking, and code-fix operations.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol \"(\" field_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION)?", "semantics": "UTTER records exactly one non-effectful symbolic proposition and is legal only in a TURN. The chosen intent schema fixes fields and order. Its optional result binds the complete proposition as PROPOSITION for later symbolic reference; it never binds prose content. Use UTTER to record a participant's request, question, correction, greeting, or social inquiry; use GENERATE for a requested artifact and ACTION for an external effect.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Can you briefly explain current New Zealand politics to me?", "braincode": "UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_new_zealand_politics,a_audience=role_user,a_depth=val_brief,a_freshness=val_current)->$v1:PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records a structured conversational act without quoted dialogue. Its meaning is an ordered glossary-defined intent proposition.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This removes prose dialogue while preserving content, recipient, register-related values, and conversational follow-up references.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= GENERATE \"(\" \"a_specification\" \"=\" proposition (\",\" \"a_reference\" \"=\" HANDLE)? \")\" \"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION", "semantics": "GENERATE is pure symbolic artifact derivation. a_specification is required and must be int_generate_artifact or an artifact-specification intent accepted by that schema; a_reference optionally names a visible PROPOSITION. The result is always PROPOSITION. GENERATE is selected when the request asks the agent to produce a story, text, introduction, contract, schedule, plan, comparison, recommendation, or other artifact; UTTER merely records that the request was made.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Write a 100-word text about my decision to go to Japan to learn about SGI.", "braincode": "GENERATE(a_specification=int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_short_text,a_specification=int_short_text_spec(a_topic=int_event(a_participants=[role_user,ent_japan,ent_sgi],a_relation=val_first_person),a_quantity=100,a_audience=role_user)))->$v1:PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate derives a requested structured artifact from a symbolic specification. It accepts and returns propositions, never natural-language text.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This makes artifact generation distinct from conversational recording while supporting constrained substantive requests symbolically.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (\":\" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= HANDLE \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= RETURN value\nstep ::= action | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation\ntask_call ::= HANDLE \"(\" field_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?", "semantics": "A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT HANDLE naming a declared TASK or CONVO. TASK contains ordered external work, control flow, binding, task calls, and symbolic generation, but never UTTER. ANCHOR is required whenever source meaning uses a relative date and carries the resolved DATE_LITERAL. Parameters, task names, calls, and results use handles only; calls use declared ordered fields and exact types.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Fix LogisticRegressionCV multinomial scoring so LogisticRegression receives multi_class.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n ACTION op_modify_code(a_path=@psklearn/linear_model/logistic.py,a_change=code_insert(a_target=code_call(a_target=code_class(a_value=code_atom_LogisticRegression),a_args=[],a_keyword_args=[]),a_value=code_keyword_arg(a_name=code_atom_multi_class,a_value=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_multi_class))),a_regression=code_test_case(a_items=[code_assert_equal(a_expected=code_boolean(a_value=code_atom_true),a_observed=code_identifier(a_value=code_atom_scoring))]))\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is an ordered executable procedure using typed handles and closed symbolic values. It is the root form for requested external work.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This removes unrestricted task and parameter identifiers while retaining typed execution and date anchoring.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= LET HANDLE \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Result sites: ACTION -> HANDLE:type; task_call -> HANDLE:type; UTTER -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION; GENERATE -> HANDLE:PROPOSITION.", "semantics": "Bind assigns a typed closed value to an opaque handle. Handles follow the global preorder allocation rule, cannot shadow visible handles, and cannot be used before their introduction. LET may copy a visible REF of exactly the same type but cannot manufacture a REF from an entity symbol, literal, proposition, or code value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Store a polite two-day extension request.", "braincode": "LET $v1:PROPOSITION=int_request_extension(a_recipient=role_manager,a_duration=2,a_unit=val_day,a_tone=val_polite)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind stores a typed formal value under a generated opaque handle. A handle is an address, not a descriptive word.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Opaque handles close the former identifier channel while preserving explicit data flow.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Iterate", "grammar": "flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable \"{\" step+ \"}\"\niterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit\nquantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check", "semantics": "FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL operate only on finite LIST[T] values. Their introduced names are handles, scoped to one loop body or quantifier check and allocated under the global handle rule. Iteration is sequential. Every list element must itself be a legal closed Types & Lexicon value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair one at a time.", "braincode": "ACTION op_find(a_target=ent_pillow,a_source=ent_sofa,a_quantity=2)->$v1:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\nFOR EACH $v2 IN $v1 { ACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY] ACTION op_place(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_armchair) }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Iterate processes a finite typed list using a formal loop handle. It cannot introduce an undocumented descriptive name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This makes iteration compatible with closed handles and reference-preserving household execution.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Check", "grammar": "check ::= disjunction\ndisjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*\nconjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*\nnegation ::= NOT negation | primary\nprimary ::= \"(\" check \")\" | quantifier | comparison\ncomparison ::= value (comp_op value)?\ncomp_op ::= \"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \">\" | \">=\"", "semantics": "Check is pure and has precedence OR, AND, NOT, comparison. Operands are only closed values. NUMBER, DATE, and TIME support all comparisons; BOOL, glossary symbols, PROPOSITION, CODE, REF, and LIST support equality and inequality only unless a schema explicitly defines ordering. Proposition equality compares its intent symbol and ordered fields recursively.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Proceed only if a reply has not arrived.", "braincode": "ACTION op_check_replied(a_target=role_manager,a_deadline=@d2025-06-06)->$v1:BOOL\nIF NOT $v1 THEN { ACTION op_wait() }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Check forms a true-or-false condition from typed closed values. It cannot inspect unknown text or identifiers.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This prevents conditions from restoring text or identifier escape hatches.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER \"=\" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? \"{\" turn_step+ \"}\"\nturn_step ::= utterance | generation\nspeaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT", "semantics": "Conversation is an ordered record of symbolic communication. TURN bodies contain UTTER and GENERATE only. REPLY_TO and REVISES name earlier TURN handles; REVISES may target only a revision chain's current tip and replaces the target's effective body. Follow-ups reference visible PROPOSITION handles through schemas that accept a_reference. Canonical selection is: use UTTER for a participant's request, use GENERATE for the requested artifact, and use ACTION only in TASK for an external effect.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Ask for great places to visit in the Netherlands.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_recommend(a_target=ent_place,a_location=ent_netherlands,a_requirement=int_property(a_target=ent_place,a_value=val_high_quality),a_quantity=10)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n TURN $v4 SPEAKER=AGENT REPLY_TO $v2 { GENERATE(a_specification=int_generate_artifact(a_artifact=val_recommendation,a_specification=$v3,a_audience=role_user),a_reference=$v3)->$v5:PROPOSITION }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records ordered symbolic requests, corrections, and generated artifacts. It uses proposition handles rather than quoted wording.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "This gives conversation, follow-up, recommendation, and generated-response requests a closed symbolic representation.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}], "_provider_used": "openai", "_model_used": "gpt-5.6-terra"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal adds useful symbolic coverage for rinse/place, retrieval/filter/sort, artifact requests, and limited code ASTs. However, several sampled closed tasks still have no operation or proposition schema (file age/deletion/archive membership, selecting a nearest store, transit schedules, movable containers), and most open substantive requests remain unrepresentable without inventing ontology schemas."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Structured propositions improve preservation of recipients, durations, ranking, and some artifact constraints. But the schemas omit essential distinctions such as email drafting versus sending, file deletion, ZIP-based proximity, colors, containment, route endpoints, revisions of supplied tables, advice constraints, and most narrative/script content; several expressions therefore cannot preserve the request."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "The proposed canonical retrieval pipeline is beneficial locally, but the language does not define operation_symbol, intent_symbol, attribute_symbol, or the category-selection rules needed to select among schemas. Independent translations already diverge materially: for the red-rag task one uses a missing red entity while another uses filter/property; for the game comparison one omits versus includes a criterion; and both providers leave major gaps for code, store, and story tasks."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Opaque handles, typed propositions, and schema-ordered fields are improvements over unrestricted strings. Yet the glossary combines dozens of supposedly independently lookupable attributes, values, operations, and schemas into bulk entries, leaves source categories and structural tokens undefined, and gives many values only an aspirational subtype statement rather than a usable per-symbol definition."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "For the rinse/place and ranked-show patterns, the decomposition would help a weaker model execute ordered work. For many other tasks the model must either fail, invent undocumented symbols, or reduce the request to a vague explain/generate proposition, which does not improve planning over the original request."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-code-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Given a list of file paths, delete every file older than 30 days unless it's in the 'archive' folder.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no schema defines file-age lookup, archive-folder membership, or file deletion.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The proposed op_remove is a household removal schema, not a filesystem deletion schema. This was also a GAP in both independent translations."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Draft a reply to my professor asking for a two-day extension on the assignment, and keep it formal.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: int_request_extension lacks an assignment/topic field, and no email-draft artifact schema distinguishes drafting a reply from sending email.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "int_request_extension helps with recipient, duration, and tone, but not the requested draft artifact or its assignment referent."}, {"item_id": "b7082615-e6e1-4981-b51b-9259671d1adf", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Change your store to the one nearest to 07055", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no store-setting operation or proximity-to-ZIP relation/schema is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "op_find/filter/sort cannot set the selected store. Gemini likewise identified the missing change-store operation."}, {"item_id": "e2adf8f1-547d-4671-96c1-4a21a56e135d", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "View the upcoming schedule from Otis St@Summer St to City Point of the transit near South Station for today.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no transit-schedule retrieval operation or route/stops/today proposition schema is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The entity category can contain station names, but there is no existing category or schema for the requested route and schedule relation."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_120422_949453#2", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Move a red rag into a sink.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n ACTION op_find(a_target=ent_red_rag,a_quantity=1)->$v2:REF[ENTITY]\n ACTION op_pick_up(a_target=$v2)->$v3:REF[ENTITY]\n ACTION op_place(a_target=$v3,a_destination=ent_sink)\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The action sequence is representable if ent_red_rag is a complete-glossary entity. It loses the compositional color/object distinction because no color attribute/value category is defined; translators may instead use ent_rag plus an invented property filter."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_165121_524545#3", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "Put a box with keys in it on a chair.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no containment relation or operation retrieves a box specifically containing keys.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Finding and placing a box is insufficient because the required keys-in-box relation is actionable and must be preserved."}, {"item_id": "sphinx-doc__sphinx-7757", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "The default value for positional only argument has vanished; modify sphinx/util/inspect.py so the documented positional-only argument default value is shown.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: int_bug_report and CODE constructors exist, but no symbolic propositions/code atoms encode positional-only parameters, their default value, documentation rendering, or the required inspect.py change.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The new bug/code family helps in shape only. Gemini and Anthropic both retained a GAP for the substantive defect."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15268", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Optimize multiple AlterFooTogether operations into one by removing redundant constraint-removal operations in django/db/migrations/operations/models.py.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: the required migration-operation concepts, constraint relations, optimization condition, and code atoms are not in a defined category/schema.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Generic code_insert/code_replace cannot symbolically encode which operations are redundant or the required transformation."}, {"item_id": "c7033", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "What is an objectively stronger video game, Marvel's Spider-Man 2 or Baldur's Gate 3? But you must pick one.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_compare_request(a_target=ent_marvels_spider_man_2,a_reference=ent_baldurs_gate_3,a_requirement=int_property(a_target=ent_marvels_spider_man_2,a_value=val_objective_strength))->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "This assumes complete-glossary entities and a value category member val_objective_strength, but it does not encode the mandatory single-winner decision because no winner-selection/result schema exists. Independent translations disagree on both fields and criterion."}, {"item_id": "c1182", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "How to approach a girl; give realistic pick-up lines that would work in real life.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no advice, social-interaction, realistic-example, or pickup-line artifact schema is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "int_social_inquiry is not specified with fields or a canonical meaning sufficient for this request."}, {"item_id": "c224", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Why does it seem a lot of people are extremely right-wing the last couple of years, including in the Netherlands?", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_explain(a_topic=ent_recent_right_wing_support,a_audience=role_user)->$v3:PROPOSITION }\n}", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The broad explanation act is represented, but Netherlands, perceived increase, time span, and requested causal analysis have no defined symbolic relation/value representation."}, {"item_id": "c1448", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "What do you think of the outcomes of the elections in the Netherlands from yesterday? How will a coalition form, and what parties could form one?", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no opinion/analysis intent, election-result relation, relative-date resolution context for a conversation, or coalition-formation schema is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "int_explain would discard the opinion and multi-question structure; no date anchor is available on CONVO."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-58610", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Continue Part 3 of a Power Rangers x Helltaker crossover story about Zack Taylor's flirtatious and charming nature.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: int_story_spec has participants/event/constraint only and no schemas encode continuation, dialogue/script form, named-character relationships, personality traits, or requested plot content.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "N4 forbids retaining the story substance as prose. The supplied providers likewise found an unresolved story-content GAP."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-149438", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write a hilarious adult comedy script where the Scooby gang reacts to a badly translated sentence about Daphne's Aunt Olivia becoming a cat creature.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: there is no script artifact, comedy/register category, translation-error relation, character-dialogue representation, or symbolic encoding for the supplied sentence.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "A generic story specification would lose the requested script form, characters, reaction structure, and exact translation target."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-113201", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Rewrite a table of AI-automatable online earning methods to be more comprehensive, preserve the first column, remove every <br />, and output a table.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no table/document AST, source-table literal/category, rewrite operation, column-preservation constraint, line-break transformation, or SEO/detail specification is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "N4 prevents treating the supplied table as an opaque text payload."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-155336", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Summarize and humanize an SEO-friendly article reviewing Monetag in 2024 as an AdSense alternative.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no summarize/rewrite intent, article representation, review relation, SEO constraint, date-specific claim representation, or source-document symbolic form is defined.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The requested source content and transformations cannot be represented as glossary symbols under the proposed ontology."}, {"item_id": "1776020181361", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "My 2017 MacBook Pro gets hot and its fan is noisy while I work. What can I do, without opening it or updating beyond its supported version?", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no troubleshooting/advice schema represents device symptoms, constraints against opening/updating, software/external remedies, or follow-up correction.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The dialogue's correction materially changes the advice; neither an applicable intent schema nor symbolic remedy vocabulary exists."}, {"item_id": "1775963744772", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "As a budget-conscious piano beginner focused on jazz and classical music, what path should I take after learning them in one month proved unrealistic?", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: no learning-plan/advice schema encodes beginner level, budget, genres, timeline feasibility, or the corrective follow-up.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "This is a multi-turn refinement, not merely an int_explain topic."}, {"item_id": "1775837106479", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Help plan a princess-themed birthday this week for a 9-year-old with 10 adults and 20 children at a play venue, then suggest gifts and invitation wording.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: schedule-spec cannot encode party planning, age, theme, venue capability, guest composition, urgency, gift suggestions, or invitation text.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The conversation includes a sequence of planning, gift, and invitation requests, none of which has a corresponding closed schema."}, {"item_id": "1775973360170", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Plan a friendly weekly routine around school until 2 PM, work from 4 PM to 8 PM, morning cereal and strong coffee, and a late-morning snack.", "braincode": "No valid BrainCode expression: schedule-spec has no time-block, recurring-week, school/work commitment, meal preference, or friendly-register schema.", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The proposed schedule artifact family is too coarse to preserve the constraints and iterative personalization."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "low", "notes": "Independent translations agree that several tasks require gaps, but they do not converge on valid encodings. Anthropic and Gemini disagree on red-rag representation and comparison fields; Gemini invents undefined operation/attribute uses for store selection; both leave code and creative-story substance unresolved. This confirms that the current schema-selection and vocabulary rules are not deterministic."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "Types & Lexicon", "change": "Define every grammar nonterminal that denotes a source-token category: operation_symbol, intent_symbol, attribute_symbol, code_constructor, intent_symbol, MONTH, DAY, HOUR, MINUTE, SECOND, DIGIT, NAME_CHAR, URL_HOST, URL_PATH, and PATH_SEGMENT. State explicitly which are metalanguage-only and provide glossary-defined generation rules for every source token form."}, {"target": "structural-symbol", "change": "Add individually lookupable entries for every fixed source token omitted from the member list, including AND, OR, NOT, and all punctuation/prefixes actually admitted by the final grammar. Reconcile the list with the exact revised grammar."}, {"target": "semantic-symbol", "change": "Replace each bulk symbol string with one glossary entry per operation, intent, attribute, controlled-value category, value, code constructor, and code atom. Each entry must specify its exact subtype, synonyms, consuming schemas, and worked example. In particular define a_deadline and every field mentioned by any operation schema."}, {"target": "value categories", "change": "Create separate value-category entries and exact member/type rules for duration-unit, tone, depth, freshness, quality, ranking, genre, relation, artifact, criterion, currency, capability, and code-AST atoms. Do not use one undifferentiated val_* list with retrospective claims that consuming schemas determine its type."}, {"target": "schema-selection table", "change": "Publish a complete canonical construct-selection table covering TASK versus CONVO, UTTER versus GENERATE, draft versus send, recommend versus explain versus compare, scalar versus list retrieval, entity compound versus entity-plus-property filter, source versus location, and all artifact-specification families. Define canonical field ordering for every schema, including nested propositions."}, {"target": "closed-task operations", "change": "Add typed schemas and vocabulary categories needed by the sampled actionable cores: filesystem age, folder membership, delete; store selection/change and ZIP/proximity; transit route/schedule lookup; containment/content relations; and explicit color/property filtering. Define their result and effect semantics rather than repurposing household op_remove."}, {"target": "artifact and conversational ontology", "change": "Add symbolic schemas, categories, and composition rules for advice/plans, document/table rewrite, summary, review, script/dialogue, story continuation, invitations, schedules/time blocks, troubleshooting, and multi-turn corrections. Each must encode the request substance symbolically under N4, not merely label it as a generic artifact."}, {"target": "code-form and SWE coverage", "change": "Extend CODE and bug-report propositions with symbolic representations for function signatures, positional-only arguments, defaults, documentation rendering, migration operations, constraints, sequences, deletion/reduction, and expected transformations. Add only atomic code-token categories with exact membership and consuming rules."}, {"target": "handles and declaration grammar", "change": "Specify whether ENTRYPOINT's handle introduces a declaration or references a later declaration, then give a non-circular allocation algorithm consistent with 'monotonically in preorder source introduction order.' Define handle allocation across task declarations, parameters, turns, loop variables, and result sites."}, {"target": "proposal examples", "change": "Revalidate every example against the final grammar and exact glossary categories. Fix unsupported source forms such as action's undefined operation_symbol, int_genre's a_value despite the grouped attribute list's incomplete field typing, code examples whose CODEATOM typing is unspecified, and any values that are only asserted to exist in an aspirational complete glossary."}], "logic_issues": ["The grammar uses operation_symbol, intent_symbol, attribute_symbol, code_constructor, and speaker-related categories without defining productions that connect them to glossary_symbol or a glossary category.", "The structural-symbol vocabulary omits AND, OR, and NOT even though Check admits them as source tokens.", "The revised semantic schemas use fields not listed in the attribute declaration, including a_deadline in op_check_replied.", "The semantic-symbol entry groups many distinct symbols in one member declaration, contrary to its claim that symbols are individually lookupable and contrary to N2's per-category/per-member requirement.", "The generic value grammar admits glossary_symbol without a grammar-level category/type restriction; the later statement that each schema fixes types is not a parseable or lookupable definition of each value category.", "NAME_LITERAL permits arbitrary NAME_CHAR+ but NAME_CHAR is not defined, and the semantics do not mechanically restrict names to proper-name atoms rather than arbitrary English words.", "The global preorder handle rule is incompatible or at least unspecified for ENTRYPOINT $v1 followed by TASK $v1: the first occurrence appears to use the handle before the declaration that introduces it.", "Task calls and declaration handles have no declared task-signature registry or handle-to-task resolution procedure, so a HANDLE(...) step is not statically resolvable.", "The proposal claims every schema fixes defaults, but no default values or default application rules are supplied.", "Several worked examples rely on unlisted exact symbols, including ent_new_zealand_politics, ent_place, ent_netherlands, ent_japan, ent_sgi, val_objective_strength, and val_formal; N6 allows a complete glossary to contain members only after the category/subtype/consuming-schema rules are complete, which they are not here."], "notes_assessment": {"N1": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Removing STRING, unrestricted IDENT, and quoted prose is real progress. N1 is still violated because valid source grammar references undefined token categories and lexical components, structural tokens are missing from the glossary, and bulk entries do not permit direct lookup of every used symbol."}, "N2": {"status": "partial", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The new entity, role, semantic, and structural vocabulary categories are substantial progress over construct-only glossary entries. The glossary remains incomplete because attributes, values, operations, schemas, and code atoms are grouped rather than individually defined, and several token families have no category entry at all."}, "N3": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The proposal moves toward typed controlled values, but it does not define a separate allowed value set for each attribute. A generic value can be any glossary_symbol/HANDLE/list/proposition/code, while the claimed schema restrictions and value subcategories are not individually enumerated or mechanically defined."}, "N4": {"status": "upheld", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The revised grammar removes quoted strings and prose payloads, and the intended atomic literals are non-phrase forms. This makes meaningful progress even though the missing symbolic ontology means many requests must currently fail rather than be encoded."}, "N5": {"status": "violated", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "Handles and literal prefixes are a useful self-contained direction. NAME_LITERAL's undefined NAME_CHAR+ rule remains an unrestricted natural-language-token channel, and multiple source token categories are undefined rather than being closed glossary rules."}, "N6": {"status": "partial", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The grammar is increasingly framed over entity, role, semantic, and code categories, so missing members within a complete category need not be penalized. Missing category-level schemas remain widespread, and the incomplete subtype/consuming-schema rules make the complete-glossary assumption unusable for many required members."}, "N7": {"status": "violated", "trend": "regressed", "reasoning": "Some ordering rules and retrieval canonicalization were added, but the current proposal introduces unresolved category selection, undefined token nonterminals, and broad schema overlap without a complete selection table. The supplied independent translations demonstrate non-convergence, so this worsens the prior determinism position."}, "N8": {"status": "partial", "trend": "improved", "reasoning": "The ontology attempts significantly richer propositions, artifacts, and code trees. Nonetheless, its current missing schemas force loss or failure on most sampled requests, and the bulk value categories do not yet safely realize richness within determinism."}}, "rebuttal_rulings": [], "chosen_position": null, "position_comparison": null, "reasoning": "The revision makes genuine progress toward closed vocabulary by removing free strings and adding symbolic categories, so its core direction is sound. It cannot be accepted because key grammar categories and fixed tokens are still undefined, the glossary is not individually lookupable, and most sampled requests lack symbolic schemas under the no-prose constraint. The deterministic-selection and handle-resolution gaps additionally regress hard note N7."}
Doc hygiene: All changes carry a complete glossary entry (gloss + worked example), and every revise/remove targets an existing entry.
Decision: needs-rework


--- RESPONSE ---
{"summary":"Sprint 13 (steering note N1, closed vocabulary) proposed replacing free strings/identifiers with handles, atomic literals, and glossary-governed schemas across Types & Lexicon, Action, Utterance, Generate, Task, Bind, Iterate, Check, and Conversation, plus new structural/entity/role/semantic-symbol vocabulary categories; the Shaper fully complied with N1 rather than contesting it. The Critic ruled needs-rework: while it credited real progress (removing STRING/IDENT/prose, adding typed propositions), it found the grammar still references undefined nonterminals (operation_symbol, intent_symbol, attribute_symbol, etc.), bulk-grouped glossary entries violate individual-lookup requirements, and simulated examples (e.g. file-deletion, store-selection, transit-schedule, and creative-writing tasks) mostly failed coverage or diverged nondeterministically across independent translations, which the Critic weighted most heavily as a regression on hard note N7 (determinism)."}

